                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I'm honestly not sure whether to recommend this game or not. \n\nI enjoyed cracking the codes. They require some thinking outside of the typical puzzle RPG box. However, some of them have obscure enough elements that a lot of people probably wouldn't even think of or know about.\n\nI enjoyed uncovering the story. The pieces reveal themselves at a good pace and you can start to see how they might fit together. However, the game promises an explanation at the climax but never actually delivers one. I actually couldn't believe it when the credits rolled. It was just so unexpected. For this reason alone, I chose to not recommend this game.\n\nOverall, the game has a very unpolished feel.\n- There are several bugs that allow you to re-access previously cleared areas. Doing so often breaks the game.\n- Spelling and grammar errors are prolific. \n- There's not a lot of life in the dialogue and text. Interacting with the world and NPCs (outside of cracking the codes) often feels pointless.  \n- Map design is terrible. Entrances and exits aren't always obvious. Pretty much all the maps are needlessly large, repetitive and tedious to traverse (especially given that exploring is almost completely unnecessary). While this actually fits somewhat with the tone of the story (reflecting the character's confusion and dysphoria), it doesn't feel like this was done intentionally. \n- Buying gear and items is available to you, but is completely unnecessary. They don't get used and there are no battles.\n\n\nIf you like cracking codes, then sure give it a try. If you're after a more typical RPG, this game definitely isn't for you.\n",
